issue_context.body:
I plan to try to resolve this issue, I just wanted to document my thought process for posterity sake.

This is observed on `sway version 1.6.1` on NixOS, using a WACOM PTH-660 (Intuos Pro):

```
Bus 003 Device 007: ID 056a:0357 Wacom Co., Ltd PTH-660 [Intuos Pro (M)]
```

I'm pretty sure, however, that it's applicable to _all_ graphics tablets, since the advent of graphics tablet support. It was caused in my original PR (which, honestly, has proven to be very shoddy&mdash;sorry about that.) I strongly believe there are no relevant log messages, so I will not include any logs.

## Analyzing the Problem

The suspect code is [here](https://github.com/swaywm/sway/blob/440d0bc/sway/input/cursor.c#L821):

```c
	if (!surface || !wlr_surface_accepts_tablet_v2(tablet_v2, surface)) {
		// TODO: the user may want to configure which tool buttons are mapped to
		// which simulated pointer buttons
		switch (event->state) {
		case WLR_BUTTON_PRESSED:
			if (cursor->tool_buttons == 0) {
				dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, &event->tablet->base,
						event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
			}
			cursor->tool_buttons++;
			break;
		case WLR_BUTTON_RELEASED:
			if (cursor->tool_buttons == 1) {
				dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, &event->tablet->base,
						event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
			}
			cursor->tool_buttons--;
			break;
		}
		wlr_seat_pointer_notify_frame(cursor->seat->wlr_seat);
		return;
	}
```

My reading of the code is as follows:

- If there is no surface found at the given cursor's coordinates, _or_ if the surface found at the given cursor's coordinates does not accept tablet v2 events, we fall back to dispatching right click events for all tool button events.
- In order to keep things consistent, we keep a count of how many tool buttons are currently pressed, `tool_buttons`. That way, we can avoid generating multiple events when multiple tool buttons are pressed at once.
- If the count falls out of sync, we practically stop generating events. For example, if we manage to get two `WLR_BUTTON_RELEASED` events after just one `WLR_BUTTON_PRESSED` event, we wind up with a `tool_buttons` value of `0xffffffff`. If the events stay consistent after that, and you only press one tool button at a time, events will never get generated.
- In theory, the count can't fall out of sync as long as this can never happen.

**However**, there are a couple of glaring problems that I'm not sure how I didn't notice when I introduced this bug:

1. One interesting problem is that this, I believe, effectively breaks resizing surfaces that _do_ handle tablet v2 events, because the cursor will _never_ generate `BTN_RIGHT` events when on top of the surface in question.
2. The `tool_buttons` count can fall out of sync in _numerous_ ways. I'm sure it's plainly obvious to anyone reading, but the most trivial way for it to fall out of sync is for the condition to become false in between `WLR_BUTTON_PRESSED` and `WLR_BUTTON_RELEASED`. So even if we receive a consistent stream of events, it can still fall out of sync due to this conditional. (The logic was correct prior to my PR, #4570.)

## Solutions

In order to try to resolve the problem, I made a quick patch to my local Sway derivation that looks like this:

```diff
diff --git a/sway/input/cursor.c b/sway/input/cursor.c
index c89d5166..59d52a8a 100644
--- a/sway/input/cursor.c
+++ b/sway/input/cursor.c
@@ -795,23 +795,21 @@ static void handle_tool_button(struct wl_listener *listener, void *data) {
 	node_at_coords(cursor->seat, cursor->cursor->x, cursor->cursor->y,
 		&surface, &sx, &sy);
 
-	if (!surface || !wlr_surface_accepts_tablet_v2(tablet_v2, surface)) {
+	struct wlr_keyboard *keyboard = wlr_seat_get_keyboard(cursor->seat->wlr_seat);
+	uint32_t modifiers = keyboard ? wlr_keyboard_get_modifiers(keyboard) : 0;
+	bool mod_pressed = modifiers & config->floating_mod;
+
+	if (!surface || !wlr_surface_accepts_tablet_v2(tablet_v2, surface) || mod_pressed) {
 		// TODO: the user may want to configure which tool buttons are mapped to
 		// which simulated pointer buttons
 		switch (event->state) {
 		case WLR_BUTTON_PRESSED:
-			if (cursor->tool_buttons == 0) {
-				dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, event->device,
-						event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
-			}
-			cursor->tool_buttons++;
+			dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, event->device,
+					event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
 			break;
 		case WLR_BUTTON_RELEASED:
-			if (cursor->tool_buttons == 1) {
-				dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, event->device,
-						event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
-			}
-			cursor->tool_buttons--;
+			dispatch_cursor_button(cursor, event->device,
+				event->time_msec, BTN_RIGHT, event->state);
 			break;
 		}
 		wlr_seat_pointer_notify_frame(cursor->seat->wlr_seat);
```

This attempt is very rough and was mainly just a one-off shot in the dark, but it _seems_ to do exactly what I wanted, though it has a couple of issues:

- This way of handling events makes no attempts to smooth over the case where multiple tool buttons are held down.
- If you let go of the modifier key first while on top of a tablet v2 capable surface, you will be stuck in resize mode as it will stop emulating mouse events.

Therefore, I think a more robust way to handle it would be to adjust the conditional _just a bit more_: In addition to turning on mouse emulation for tool buttons when holding down the floating mod key, we should also keep mouse emulation on until `tool_buttons` reaches zero. In addition, instead of allowing the `tool_buttons` value to underflow, we should log a warning and refuse to lower the `tool_buttons` value if `WLR_BUTTON_RELEASED` occurs when `tool_buttons` is already zero, probably in addition to dispatching the cursor button release. While this only handles half of the cases, this should be able to provide some diagnostics should there be a similar logic error introduced in the future, or issues with events being inconsistent.

(edit: It may also be worth refactoring the floating resize events so that they can simply understand the native tablet events instead, however, I think that this is worth holding off for a future enhancement. For now, simply enabling mouse click emulation is a relatively safe solution.)

review_comments:
To make this logic a little easier to read, can we write it as

```c
if (cursor->tool_buttons == 0) {
   ...
} else if (cursor->tool_buttons <= 1) {
   ...
} else {
   ...
}
```

I think writing it this way also makes the `<= 1` case impossible to ever be `<` (because we won't be decrementing when `cursor->tool_buttons = 0` to get into this branch in the first place)?

In which case we might just need

```c
if (cursor->tool_buttons == 0) {
    sway_log(SWAY_ERROR, "inconsistent tablet tool button events");
} else {
    cursor->tool_buttons--;
}
```

What do you think?
